      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Nashville and Northwestern Railroad ran through Gleason in 1860, sparking rapid growth and connecting the town to regional markets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.utm.edu/academics/library/special-collections/wc_hist/gleason.php",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Mid‑1930s clay deposits turned Gleason into a major pottery center, and the industry remains an economic backbone.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.utm.edu/academics/library/special-collections/wc_hist/gleason.php",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Every fall, Gleason hosts the Tater Town Parade and festival, drawing visitors to its quirky potato‑themed celebrations.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gleason%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Gleason is the hometown of Gordon Stoker, the first tenor of the legendary backup group The Jordanaires.",
